To design a group-fair count-min sketch that is fair for all groups, we propose two novel techniques in this paper. Firstly, we introduce the concept of semi-uniform hashing and its connection to our problem. Then, we present our main contributions as follows:\n\n1) We define the notion of 
Group Fairness (Definition 2). A count-min sketch is group-fair if it satisfies Definition  cuis equal multiplicative error across various groups. Specifically, a CM is called fair if it satisfies:
Parent: 

The solution to this problem involves designing a group-fair count-min sketch that ensures an equal approximation factor for all individual element types and prevents disproportionate overestimation of low-frequency elements in the sketch. This can be achieved by using semi-uniform hashing schemes, which strategically partition and assign columns to different groups based on their group membership. We prove that our proposed solution satisfies Definition 2 (group fairness) with a small increase in the overall additive error compared to CM.
